1. Capture the Event While Details Are Fresh
The first report should establish what happened, where it happened, the task underway and any immediate response. Photographs or supporting records can help when they are safe and appropriate to collect.

Keep the initial form manageable. A worker reporting a close call should not have to complete an investigator’s entire questionnaire.

Immediate assistance and control of an ongoing danger take priority over entering information into software.

2. Review Potential Consequences
An event without injury may still involve serious exposure.

Review both the actual outcome and what could reasonably have happened. A dropped object beside an occupied work area, for example, needs attention even if it caused no damage.

Decide who should review the event, what specialist input is needed and whether temporary controls are sufficient while investigation continues.

AI-generated classifications can assist that review. A responsible person should confirm the priority using the evidence and operating context.

3. Investigate Beyond the First Explanation
“Worker error” rarely provides enough detail to guide effective improvement.

Examine the equipment, instructions, workspace, supervision and conditions surrounding the task. Ask whether the procedure was practical and whether the team had suitable tools, time and information.

OSHA’s investigation guidance emphasises looking beyond immediate causes and examining the circumstances that allowed an event to occur.

NeoEHS describes support for structured methods including 5 Why, Fishbone and Fault Tree Analysis. These tools help organise an investigation; the conclusions still need to be supported by evidence.

4. Assign Actions That Address the Findings
An action should explain what will change, who owns it and when it is due.

Compare these two entries:

“Remind employees to be careful.”

“Restore the designated pedestrian route, review the storage arrangement and verify that the route remains accessible during peak operations.”

The second provides something concrete to implement and check.

NeoEHS supports action assignment, progress tracking and escalation, helping teams maintain visibility over outstanding work.

5. Verify Before Closing
Completion and effectiveness are different questions.

A photograph may show that a barrier was installed. A follow-up observation can establish whether people and vehicles remain separated during normal work.

Agree on the evidence needed for closure and, where appropriate, schedule a later effectiveness review. If the same issue returns, revisit the original finding and the action selected.

Where AI Can Add Practical Value
AI assistance is particularly useful when information is spread across large numbers of reports.

It can help reviewers explore whether differently worded observations describe similar events, locate potentially related records and identify patterns that deserve closer examination.

For example, reports describing “restricted access,” “materials across the walkway” and “pedestrians using the vehicle lane” may concern a common layout problem.

That relationship is a hypothesis to investigate. It becomes useful only when someone checks the location, the records and the circumstances.

Treat predictive scores in the same way: as signals requiring interpretation. A model trained on incomplete reporting may overlook risks that workers have never recorded.

An Illustrative Example: A Loading-Bay Near Miss
A worker steps around stored pallets and enters a forklift route. The driver brakes before contact.

The immediate response addresses the exposure. The investigation then examines why the worker left the pedestrian route.

The team discovers that temporary deliveries regularly occupy the walkway during a shift change. Earlier reports described the same area using different terms, so the pattern was difficult to recognise.

Potential actions include changing delivery staging, restoring the route and reviewing the layout with the people who use it. A follow-up check during the busy period tests whether the revised arrangement works.

